# Concept: sealed fields and custody
#
# `sealed` fields are encrypted before they reach the database.
#   - Each tenant has a master-wrapped key, and the data key has numbered
#     epochs. A sealed value carries the epoch it was written under.
#   - `ordo keys init` mints a tenant's custody. `ordo keys rotate` mints the
#     next epoch and a worker re-seals old rows. `ordo keys destroy` retires an
#     old epoch for good.
#   - The command log never holds a sealed value, only an opaque handle.
#   - Blind-index keys come from the tenant master key, not the epoch, so a
#     rotation never moves a digest and `unique` stays true.
#   - Deleting a tenant destroys its key (crypto-erasure).
#
# Read next: docs/howto-custody.md, docs/glossary.md "DEK epoch / rotation".

domain clinic @ 1.0.0

entity patients {
  ward:      text
  admitted:  date
  name:      text   sealed                              # encrypted
  email:     text?  sealed indexed unique normalize(lower)
  diagnosis: text   sealed
}

# Create or update a patient by email. The key is the blind index digest.
op upsertPatient($ward: text, $admitted: date, $name: text, $email: text, $diagnosis: text) =
  set patients { email: $email, ward: $ward, admitted: $admitted, name: $name, diagnosis: $diagnosis }

# Lookup by email uses the blind index: SQL speed, no key in the database.
op wardOf($email: text) =
  patients(email == $email)[ward, admitted]

# Projecting sealed fields opens them host-side, one row at a time.
op recentPatients =
  patients[name, diagnosis, admitted]
  sort -admitted
  take 10
